  "notes": "Digitized from the Kodak E-4021 datasheet of September 2008. Kodak: \"slightly higher contrast and color saturation\" than PORTRA ENDURA; rolls \"discontinued by year end 2009\", SUPRA ENDURA VC Digital being \"the alternative replacement\". The sheet prints one spectral sensitivity and one dye density plot for PORTRA ENDURA and SUPRA ENDURA together; both profiles carry them. Status A curves for a 0.5 s sensitometric exposure; the sheet does not say what light the sensitometer used, so the enlarger here is the same stand-in as ENDURA Premier's. No granularity and no MTF on the sheet. The x axis's -1.0 tick is drawn 0.25 log H right of where the frame and the -2.0 tick put it (a drafting error, like the label errors in the film sheets), so x is calibrated on the frame, whose ends carry the -3.0 and 0.0 labels; on the -2.0 tick instead the log H scale would be 1 percent longer. R, G and B by D-max, the order the sheet's labels stand in at the curves' ends.",
  "source": {
    "publication": "E-4021",
    "edition": "2008-09",
    "url": "https://125px.com/docs/paper/kodak/e4021.pdf",
    "digitized": "2026-10-08",
    "method": "vector paths of the datasheet PDF read with pypdf 6.19.0 by bake/tools/paper_from_sheet.py (Beziers flattened at 64 samples per segment), axes calibrated by least squares on the labelled tick and grid lines (largest residual 0.2989 axis units), curves resampled by linear interpolation every 0.05 log H and 5 nm; the same script reads E-4070 within 0.0007 Status A density, 0.0004 log S and 0.0004 D of the reviewed ENDURA Premier profile; on a Quartz rendering of the page the curves lie a median 0.05 pt (99th percentile 0.08 pt) from the ink",
    "labelErrors": "The characteristic plot's x axis -1.0 tick is drawn 0.25 log H right of its place."
  },
